> is x odd?
  note: "odd": read as an odd function, f(-x) = -f(x), not an odd number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(x) = x
  ucas: parity it
parity of it:
  odd: f(-x) = -f(x)
  proved symbolically (parity rules: x is odd; sums keep a common parity, products multiply it; sin/tan/atan preserve it, cos/abs make it even)
> is z odd?
  note: "odd": read as an odd function, f(-x) = -f(x), not an odd number for every input (say "an odd function" or "always odd")
  ucas: it(z) = z
  ucas: parity it
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
<english>:2:1: error: parity works on real functions; this one is complex (use re, im, modulus or arg to get a real one, or define it with real parameters)
    parity it
    ^
